# What must never be uploaded to a Vercel deployment.
#
# `vercel deploy` from a terminal uploads the working directory, and unlike a git-based deploy
# it does not honour .gitignore. The first CLI deploy of this project therefore carried the
# local `.env` — live database credentials, the service-role key, the admin password — into the
# build, where Next loaded it ("Environment variables loaded from .env" in the build log) and
# baked it into the serverless bundle.
#
# That worked, which was the problem: the site came up, so nothing looked wrong, while the
# secrets sat in a deployment artifact instead of Vercel's encrypted environment store. It also
# broke the admin portal in a way that looked like a rejected password — a bundled .env reaches
# the Node runtime but NOT the edge runtime, so `/api/admin/login` could sign a session token
# that `middleware.ts` could not verify, and every /dashboard request bounced back to the
# sign-in screen.
#
# Every variable the app needs is now a real Vercel environment variable, which is injected
# into both runtimes. This file is what stops a stray .env from quietly overriding that
# arrangement on the next CLI deploy.
.env
.env.*

# Local build output and caches. Vercel builds from source; uploading these wastes the upload
# and can confuse the build with a stale .next from a different Next version.
.next
node_modules
coverage

# Local-only tooling artefacts that have no business in a deployment.
.playwright-mcp
prisma/backups
*.log
